shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | Adds the 78→86 job-count drift from 2026-09-15, restating the tell in its threshold-free form (single-digit okCount = SSD absent) | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Tracking session transcripts added ~368 MB to `~/.claude` and made repo size grow daily; left as an open decision because Tier B capture is a proven reconstruction fallback | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] | Multi-task Mac+Windows skills/backup plan: 42 own vs 19 third-party skills reframing, Task 4 inversion script deleted, Task 2 prune 306→208 as a reversible move, three open round-10 findings | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] | A `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matches directories but not symlinks, so the rule silently no-ops and an automated backup re-adds the entries; plus `.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` scope | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] | Restore test returned 57 of 61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`; only an actual restore can measure coverage the backup's own records cannot | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] | A rate-limit-forced switch from Codex to Claude found the plan's biggest error; resume cannot cross providers, and a prior approval is stale after an amendment (5 new bugs in the amendment itself) | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] | Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file, failing only while the SSD is mounted; fixed by rename, `okCount: 80` | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | Adds the Codex-skills symlink refusal as a second rsync type-change instance, making recurrence the argument for fixing the tooling | 2026-09-12.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways]] | Replacing a failed check with a primitive substrate works only where a ground truth exists; otherwise the only lever is a peer with uncorrelated blind spots. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] | Session lifetime and mount state both silently shrink what a run attempts without producing an error, and both are fixed by moving the verdict onto a durable artifact that publishes cardinality. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact]] | A failed upload or render usually indicts the transport layer, not the file — hold the artifact fixed and remove a stage before rebuilding anything | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] | Windows laptop had no working backup: ~15 GB single-copy, nine Disabled tasks, two triggerless ones, and the Mac's manifest installed as Windows'; `/MIR`→`/E` fix | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] | Four Task Scheduler gotchas: `schtasks /Change` can't add a schedule, triggerless tasks show `Ready` forever, `G:` is per-user so SYSTEM writes nothing, sleeping laptops miss fixed-time triggers | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] | Partition cross-machine repair by capability: author all file edits from the working machine, hand the broken one a paste-ready instruction page and in-file \"nothing reads this yet\" notes | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] | A received recovery report's findings held but one explanation didn't: `dev-sync` skips an enumerated list, not underscore-prefixed folders, so `_INBOX` was already a clean pushed repo | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] | Three Windows pushes mid-session each needing a merge, including a deliberate-looking 913-file skill audit cutting ~160→63; accepted because git retains deletions, intent still to be confirmed | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] | `Ready`, `status: OK`, and a backup's own records were each locally truthful while coverage failed; narrow stage predicates don't compose into end-to-end assurance | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] | Codex skills converted to symlinks while the SSD held real directories; rsync cannot replace a directory with a symlink, fixed by moving four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] | Six Baylor-sounding repos (`VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, …) were personal, not work; manifest classification must be asked, not inferred | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] | `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error/86-job re-run — fixes are not their own confirmation | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]] | `LibraryProject`'s embedded `ZoteroInjestion` one commit behind the standalone repo; `STATUS.md` missing its Next section with an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/system-health-check-remediation.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code System Health Check and Remediation Session\"\naliases: [system health check, memsearch timeout raise, memory injection audit, Tier A index trimming, memsearch Milvus healthy, auto-mode blocks .claude edits, claude-agent-sdk upgrade, errors log cleanup, MEMORY.md index trim]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, tooling, maintenance, memsearch, milvus, infrastructure, configuration]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-25.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-01\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# Claude Code System Health Check and Remediation Session\n\nOn 2026-06-25, a system health-check and remediation session worked through four flagged maintenance items — memsearch/Milvus timeout tuning, memory-compiler SDK upgrade, calendar auth, and a memory-injection audit — plus a cleanup of the resolved-error noise in `.errors.log`. The session raised memsearch's timeout thresholds, upgraded `claude-agent-sdk`, trimmed the Tier A auto-injection index from 15 to 10 files for token savings, and cleared an overdue pending decision from global memory.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **memsearch timeout raised, two layers in sync:** the internal timeout in `memsearch-sync.js` was raised 25s→45s, and the `settings.json` hook's outer limit was raised 28s→50s to match. The outer hook limit must exceed the internal timeout or the hook is killed before memsearch can complete.\n- **claude-agent-sdk upgraded 0.2.101→0.2.110** in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler` to fix flush-failed errors — a continuation of the version-skew maintenance pattern (see [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]]).\n- **Tier A auto-injection index trimmed 15→10 files:** four files (Zotero CLI, AI Integration Initiative, Athlete Data Governance, Sport Science Symposium) were removed from auto-injection but kept on disk (still memsearch-searchable). Estimated token savings ~8,800→~6,700 injected per session.\n- **Milvus was healthy the whole time:** the ETIMEDOUT errors were a memsearch spawn/timeout issue, not a Milvus outage — the obvious culprit (the vector DB) was not the cause. Milvus ran healthy in Docker throughout.\n- **Overdue pending decision cleared:** the stale \"memory injection cost audit\" pending decision was removed from global MEMORY.md and replaced with an OKR Q1 gate review dated 8/31; `.errors.log` was cleared of resolved ETIMEDOUT noise from 6/12–6/24.\n\n## Details\n\nThe session illustrates a recurring maintenance discipline: periodically working through flagged health-check items in a single dedicated pass rather than letting them accumulate. The memsearch timeout tuning addressed the ETIMEDOUT noise that had been accumulating in `.errors.log` since mid-June (see [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] for the concurrent-contention ETIMEDOUT pattern). Critically, the fix required adjusting two coordinated limits — the internal memsearch timeout and the outer hook timeout in `settings.json` — keeping the outer limit strictly larger so the hook does not terminate memsearch mid-operation. The diagnostic lesson was to not assume the obvious culprit: Milvus, the vector database memsearch depends on, was healthy in Docker the entire time; the timeouts originated in memsearch's own spawn/timeout behavior.\n\nThe Tier A index trimming is a token-efficiency operation on the memory-injection layer. The global MEMORY.md indexes Tier A project files for auto-injection at session start; as that index grows, each session pays a larger fixed token cost. Trimming the index from 15 to 10 files (removing four lower-priority project files from auto-injection while leaving them on disk) reduced the estimated per-session injection cost from ~8,800 to ~6,700 tokens. Because the removed files remain on disk and memsearch-searchable, no information is lost — only the always-on injection footprint is reduced, following the same Tier A/Tier B capacity-management logic documented in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]]. A notable operational constraint surfaced during the session: the auto-mode classifier blocks edits to files under `.claude/` (including `settings.json` and per-project MEMORY.md files) even when those files are personal notes rather than active configuration. Such edits require an explicit user \"go ahead,\" and some operations (like `Clear-Content` on `.errors.log`) must be run manually by the user because the classifier will not permit the agent to perform them.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The memsearch tool whose timeout thresholds were tuned here; the ETIMEDOUT errors cleared from `.errors.log` are the same concurrent-contention pattern documented there, now addressed by raising the timeout ceilings.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The memory compiler whose `claude-agent-sdk` was upgraded 0.2.101→0.2.110 to fix flush-failed errors, continuing the version-skew maintenance pattern established there.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The three-tier memory architecture whose Tier A auto-injection index was trimmed for token savings; removed files stay memsearch-searchable, consistent with the Tier A cross-project retrievability property.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The broader Claude Code environment maintenance discipline; this session is another instance of periodically triaging flagged health-check items before they degrade session quality.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-25.md]] — System health-check session (13:29): memsearch timeout raise (25→45s internal, 28→50s hook), claude-agent-sdk upgrade (0.2.101→0.2.110), Tier A index trim 15→10 files, overdue pending decision cleared and replaced with OKR Q1 gate review (8/31), `.errors.log` cleared, Milvus-healthy diagnostic lesson, auto-mode classifier `.claude/` edit-blocking behavior.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\"\naliases: [.errors.log archival, stale error entries, error log clearing procedure, archive then clear, escalation log hygiene, live vs stale escalation]\ntags: [automation, maintenance, memory, macos, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-31.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-31\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\n\nThe `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` file is the asynchronous escalation channel through which scheduled jobs surface failures to the next interactive session. Because it is append-only and read at session start, it accumulates entries from failures that have since been fixed, and a stack-doctor pass must decide per-entry whether each one still describes a live condition. On 2026-08-31 the log's entries were confirmed stale, then archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The error log is an escalation channel, not a state record.** Scheduled tasks write to it because no session exists at run time; entries persist until manually cleared regardless of whether the underlying failure was resolved.\n- **Clearing requires a staleness determination first.** Each entry must be checked against current ground truth — the relevant job's log, the artifact it was supposed to produce — before the log is emptied, or a live failure is silently discarded.\n- **Archive, then clear.** The procedure preserves the entries elsewhere rather than deleting them outright, keeping the failure history available while restoring the log's signal value for the next session.\n- **An uncleared log degrades into noise.** Because it is surfaced at every session start, stale entries train the reader to skim past it, which is the same alarm-fatigue dynamic that motivated raising the memsearch session-end alarm threshold from 3 to 6.\n\n## Details\n\nThe escalation log exists to solve a specific structural problem: a scheduled job that fails at 06:15 or 09:00 has no session to report to, so it writes to a durable file that the next session start reads. That design makes the log write-only from the job's perspective — nothing in the pipeline knows when a failure has been remediated, so nothing removes the entry. The log therefore represents \"failures that were reported at some point,\" not \"failures that are currently true,\" and any maintenance pass over it is fundamentally a reconciliation task rather than a cleanup task.\n\nThe reconciliation is per-entry and requires an independent source. On 2026-08-31 the determination was made by checking the backup log's timeline: the entries corresponded to the 09:00 manifest failure, and the 10:31 re-run had already completed clean, so every entry described a resolved condition. Had any entry lacked such a corroborating success, clearing the log would have erased the only surviving record of an unresolved failure. Archiving before clearing hedges against a wrong staleness call — the entries remain recoverable if a later session discovers the condition was live after all — and it also preserves the failure history for pattern analysis across maintenance passes.\n\nThe cost of not doing this is not data loss but signal loss. A log that is displayed at every session start and reliably contains only old, already-fixed entries stops being read, at which point the escalation channel it implements has failed silently while appearing to work. Keeping it empty except when something is genuinely wrong is what makes its non-emptiness informative.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — Defines the stack-doctor routine and the `.errors.log` escalation mechanism that scheduled tasks write to; this article covers the maintenance of that log.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The same 2026-08-31 session; the manifest failure was the source of the entries that were confirmed stale and cleared.\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The alarm-threshold raise (3→6) that addressed the same noise-versus-signal problem from the opposite direction: making an alarm fire less often so that firing means something.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why scheduled jobs need an asynchronous escalation file at all: they run without a session to report to.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-31.md]] — Stack-doctor session (10:34): `.errors.log` entries confirmed stale rather than live, archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Memory Compiler Setup\"\naliases: [claude-memory-compiler, coleam00/claude-memory-compiler, persistent memory hooks, session-start session-end pre-compact, memory compiler version skew, claude-agent-sdk version skew, FLUSH_ERROR version skew, uv lock upgrade]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, automation, infrastructure, hooks, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-05-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-13.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-14\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Claude Memory Compiler Setup\n\nOn 2026-04-14, the `coleam00/claude-memory-compiler` system — the same daily-log-to-knowledge-base architecture this very knowledge base runs on — was installed to give Claude Code persistent memory across sessions. The repo was cloned to `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/`, Python dependencies installed via `uv sync`, and the knowledge base directory structure (`daily/`, `knowledge/concepts/`, etc.) initialized. This system is Tier C of a three-tier Claude Code memory architecture; see [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] for the full picture including Tier A (native auto-memory) and Tier B (custom Stop-hook scratchpad). On 2026-06-13, a day-long failure caused by `claude-agent-sdk` version skew revealed an important operational maintenance pattern.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Three global hooks were wired into `~/.claude/settings.json`: `session-start.py` (SessionStart), `session-end.py` (SessionEnd), and `pre-compact.py` (PreCompact).\n- Hook commands invoke `uv` via its absolute path (`~/.local/bin/uv`) rather than the bare `uv` command, because Claude Code's hook execution environment may not include `~/.local/bin` on `PATH`.\n- All script invocations follow the `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler <script>` pattern, guaranteeing the correct virtualenv context regardless of the current working directory when a hook fires.\n- Hook configuration changes are not picked up live — they require a full Claude Code restart (or a `/hooks` reload) before they take effect.\n- After 6 PM local time, the `flush.py` background process automatically triggers `compile.py` to turn that day's daily log into knowledge articles, without manual intervention. Manual queries are also available via `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler python scripts/query.py \"<question>\"`.\n- **The agent itself never receives a \"session ending\" notification** — SessionEnd and PreCompact are harness-level events; the harness runs the hook shell commands, not the agent. Teardown logic must live in the hook, not in agent-side instructions.\n- **Version skew failure mode:** the memory compiler's `uv` virtualenv pins `claude-agent-sdk` to a specific version, which bundles a specific Claude Code CLI binary. When the system Claude Code auto-updates (e.g., from 2.1.92 to 2.1.177), the bundled CLI in the venv falls behind and eventually cannot parse the current `~/.claude/settings.json`, causing every `flush.py` invocation to exit immediately with a non-zero code. Symptom: 100% FLUSH_ERROR rate after a Claude Code update, despite no changes to the memory compiler itself.\n- **Fix for version skew:** `cd ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler && uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`. This upgrades only the `claude-agent-sdk` dependency and its bundled CLI to match the current system version without touching other pinned dependencies. Run this whenever FLUSH_ERRORs appear after a Claude Code auto-update.\n- **Diagnostic caution:** do NOT invoke the bundled `claude.exe` CLI directly to diagnose version skew — it spawns a real nested agent session. Instead, inspect venv package metadata (e.g., `uv pip show claude-agent-sdk`) to check the installed version against the system CLI version.\n\n## Details\n\nThe setup session focused on making the memory pipeline reliable rather than merely functional. The PATH issue with `uv` is a generalizable lesson for any Claude Code hook that shells out to a tool installed via `uv`, `pipx`, or similar user-local installers: hook processes may run with a minimal environment, so absolute paths (or explicit `PATH` exports) are safer than relying on the interactive shell's configuration. Similarly, the `uv run --directory` pattern avoids a whole class of \"wrong virtualenv\" bugs that would otherwise occur if a hook fires while Claude Code's working directory is some unrelated project.\n\nOnce restarted, the three hooks form a closed loop: `session-start.py` injects the current knowledge index and the most recent daily log into every new session's context (capped at 20,000 characters), `session-end.py` and `pre-compact.py` capture the raw conversation transcript and spawn a detached `flush.py` process, and `flush.py` decides what's worth appending to the day's daily log and, after 6 PM, kicks off `compile.py` to synthesize that log into structured `knowledge/` articles. PreCompact serves as a safety net for long sessions: in a session with multiple auto-compactions, PreCompact fires before each one, ensuring at least one compiled log exists even if the session later crashes before SessionEnd fires.\n\nThe 2026-06-13 version skew failure is operationally important. On that day, Claude Code auto-updated from approximately version 2.1.92 to 2.1.177 — 85 releases ahead — while the memory compiler's venv still bundled the older CLI via `claude-agent-sdk==0.1.56`. The newer `~/.claude/settings.json` included hooks, settings, or schemas added after version 2.1.92 that the stale bundled CLI could not parse; every `flush.py` call exited on startup without processing the session. The failure was isolated entirely to the Tier C compile layer: Tier B (the `transcript-capture.js` Stop-hook) continued capturing sessions uninterrupted because it has no dependency on `claude-agent-sdk`. Twenty-four days of continuous Tier B logs and four sessions captured on the failure day itself confirmed the architectural independence. This means that during a version-skew failure, raw session context is preserved in Tier B transcripts and can be backfilled after the fix is applied. The `uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync` fix is surgical — it upgrades only the affected package without touching other pinned dependencies — and is safe to apply at any time. A post-fix test flush verifies the fix before trusting that normal operation has resumed. See [[connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure]] for the architectural insight this failure demonstrated empirically.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The three-tier framing that situates this compiler as Tier C, alongside Tier A (native auto-memory) and Tier B (custom Stop-hook per-turn scratchpad).\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — A later (2026-06-10) maintenance pass on the same Claude Code environment, addressing plugin/MCP configuration drift in the broader tooling this memory system runs inside.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — Another automation pipeline (built 2026-06-10) sharing the same token-efficiency motivation: pre-process inputs so the LLM only has to read a compact summary.\n- [[connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure]] — Documents the 2026-06-13 empirical demonstration that Tier B (transcript capture) remained fully operational through a day-long Tier C (compiler) failure, validating the architecture's independence claim under real failure conditions.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Initial install, hook wiring, and PATH/uv lessons from the setup session.\n- [[daily/2026-05-19.md]] — Clarification of hook firing mechanics and the \"agent never sees session ending\" architectural detail (16:53).\n- [[daily/2026-06-13.md]] — Version skew failure and fix session (17:26 Memory Flush): root cause (claude-agent-sdk==0.1.56 bundling CLI 2.1.92 vs system 2.1.177), Tier B independence confirmed (24 days continuous logs), fix via `uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`, diagnostic caution about invoking bundled claude.exe.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/project-docs-file-map-pattern.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Project Docs File-Map Pattern: API Surfaces in CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md\"\naliases: [file map section, simulator file map, API surface docs, exported symbol list, context-window cost reduction, SimulatorClient API surface, loadCalc API, syncPractice API]\ntags: [claude-code, documentation, workflow, tooling, architecture, context-efficiency]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-28.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-28\nupdated: 2026-06-15\n---\n\n# Project Docs File-Map Pattern: API Surfaces in CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md\n\nOn 2026-05-28, a \"Simulator file map\" section was added to the Baylor Load Simulator's `AGENTS.md`, documenting the exported API surfaces of `SimulatorClient.tsx`, `loadCalc.ts`, and `syncPractice.ts`. The intent is to let future sessions answer \"what does this file export and how do I call it?\" directly from the project docs, without opening and re-reading the source file — reducing context-window cost and speeding up session start-up when returning to an established codebase.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- A **file map section** inside `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` lists, for each key source file: the file path, its primary exports (component name, function signatures, type definitions), and usage notes (props, required arguments, return values, side effects).\n- The pattern targets files that are called frequently across sessions but rarely change structurally — candidate files are the \"integration points\" of a codebase: API clients, calculation libraries, sync orchestration functions.\n- On 2026-05-28, three Baylor Load Simulator files were documented this way: `SimulatorClient.tsx` (the React client component's prop interface), `loadCalc.ts` (the load calculation function signature and parameters), and `syncPractice.ts` (the sync orchestration entry point and its options shape).\n- This pattern is additive to the `@AGENTS.md` import stub approach (see [[concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files]]): the stub ensures Claude Code reads `AGENTS.md`, and the file-map section inside `AGENTS.md` provides the symbol-level detail that makes re-reading source files unnecessary in routine sessions.\n- The file-map section belongs under a clearly labeled heading (e.g., `## Simulator file map`) and should be updated whenever a documented API surface changes — treating it as live documentation rather than a one-time snapshot.\n\n## Details\n\nThe context-window cost of re-reading source files in each session is non-trivial for an established codebase: a single read of `SimulatorClient.tsx` to locate a component's prop interface may consume hundreds of tokens and require the session to read several hundred lines of JSX before reaching the relevant export. A file-map entry for the same component — two to five lines listing the prop names, types, and defaults — answers the question in under 50 tokens. Across a session that touches three or four integration points, the accumulated savings can shift a modest portion of the context budget toward productive work rather than orientation.\n\nThe pattern works best for files with stable, narrow, well-defined API surfaces: data-model classes, utility functions, API client adapters, and calculation libraries are good candidates; broad page components with many internal sub-components, or files under active refactoring, are poor candidates (the map would go stale quickly). A practical heuristic: if a file has been read by the assistant in two or more consecutive sessions primarily to look up a function signature or a component's expected inputs, it belongs in the file map. The file-map section should list only the exported API surface — not implementation details, not internal helper functions — so that it remains a concise lookup index rather than an inline copy of the source. When a documented file is substantially refactored, updating the file-map entry is the same category of maintenance task as updating a `CLAUDE.md` known-gotchas section: a few minutes of documentation work that pays dividends across many future sessions.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files]] — The broader context-file strategy this file-map pattern lives inside: the `@AGENTS.md` import stub ensures Claude Code reads the file where the file map lives; the 2026-05-28 session applied both patterns in the same documentation pass.\n- [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-fall-camp-mode]] — The Baylor Load Simulator project whose `AGENTS.md` received the file-map section; the Fall Camp mode's `loadCalc` / `syncPractice` parameterization work (2026-05-26) is exactly the kind of session that benefits from pre-documented API surfaces on re-entry.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The memory compiler's `session-start.py` hook injects the knowledge-base index at session start for a similar reason: pre-loading a compact, structured summary so the session does not need to re-read raw artifacts to orient itself.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-28.md]] — File-map addition session (14:27): \"Simulator file map\" section documenting SimulatorClient.tsx, loadCalc.ts, and syncPractice.ts API surfaces added to the Baylor Load Simulator's AGENTS.md.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Compiler Backlog as a Hook-Reported Latency Signal\"\naliases: [7 logs pending 2 compiled, compiler backlog notice, self-draining backlog, backlog causes failing test, uv run compile.py, latency not loss]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, compiler, automation, maintenance, testing]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-01.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-02.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# Compiler Backlog as a Hook-Reported Latency Signal\n\nThe session-start hook reports how many daily logs remain uncompiled, surfacing the Tier C compiler's queue depth as a standing notice. On 2026-09-01 it read 7 logs pending against 2 compiled. The signal is one of latency rather than loss — the source daily logs are on disk and immutable, so a backlog delays knowledge availability without endangering the underlying material. On 2026-09-02 the backlog acquired a second consequence: it was identified as the cause of the compiler repo's one failing test, converting a passive queue-depth notice into an active blocker on the test suite.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The hook reports queue depth, not failure.** \"7 pending, 2 compiled\" describes how far behind the compile stage is; the daily logs themselves are already captured and durable.\n- **Backlogs in this system are usually self-draining.** The 54-log backlog after the machine restore drained on the nightly schedule without intervention, which is why a pending count is not automatically an action item.\n- **A backlog can nonetheless break something downstream.** On 2026-09-02 the uncompiled logs were identified as the cause of the compiler repo's single failing test, which makes draining it a prerequisite for a clean suite rather than a convenience.\n- **The manual drain is a one-liner:** `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler python scripts/compile.py`.\n- **Forcing a drain has a real cost** — each daily log compiles for roughly $0.45–0.65 — so the default of letting the nightly job absorb it is economically as well as operationally correct, absent a downstream blocker.\n\n## Details\n\nThe distinction between latency and loss is what makes the hook notice readable. Tier C's value chain runs source log → compiled knowledge article, and only the second half is behind. Because `daily/` is append-only and the compiler tracks SHA-256 hashes in `state.json`, a log that has not yet been compiled will be compiled eventually and will produce the same output whenever it runs. This is structurally unlike a dropped monitor event or an unwritten memory entry, where the material itself is gone. The correct default reading of a nonzero pending count is therefore \"knowledge is not yet queryable,\" not \"knowledge was lost\" — the same reading applied to the 54-log restore backlog in [[concepts/machine-restore-closeout-handoff]], which was explicitly classified as self-draining rather than as an action item.\n\nThe 2026-09-02 addition changes the calculus in a specific and limited way. A failing test in the compiler repo attributable to the backlog means the pending logs are now blocking a verification signal, not just delaying article availability. That is enough to justify a manual run, because a persistently red suite trains the reader to ignore it — the same alarm-fatigue dynamic that motivated raising the memsearch alarm threshold and clearing the stale `.errors.log`. The distinction to hold onto is that the backlog did not become more urgent in itself; it became coupled to something that is urgent, and that coupling is what promotes it from a notice to a task.\n\nAgainst that sits the cost. Compiling a daily log runs $0.45–0.65 and rises as the knowledge base grows, so seven pending logs is a few dollars of work that the nightly job would otherwise perform for free from the user's perspective — it happens on a schedule either way. The reasonable policy is to let the schedule handle it by default and to force a drain only when something downstream depends on the queue being empty, which is exactly the situation the failing test created.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The Tier C compiler whose queue this notice reports, including per-log costs and the `state.json` hash tracking that makes the backlog safely deferrable.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — Tier C's place among the three memory layers and its session-boundary firing model.\n- [[concepts/machine-restore-closeout-handoff]] — The 54-log backlog classified as self-draining, the precedent this notice is read against.\n- [[concepts/memlint-memory-tier-cleanup]] — The 2026-09-02 session that identified the backlog as the cause of the one failing test.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The health-check routine that treats a small compiler backlog as non-actionable given the nightly drain rate.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The same alarm-fatigue argument for keeping a standing signal meaningful.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-01.md]] — Session-start hook notice: 7 daily logs pending compilation against 2 compiled.\n- [[daily/2026-09-02.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:35): action item to run `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler python scripts/compile.py` to clear the 7-log backlog, noted as \"also causes the one failing test.\"\n\n```\n\n### concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-03.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-06-03 Run)\"\naliases: [2026-06-03 inbox triage, Catapult renewal overdue 6-03, Playfly timecard overdue 6-03, needs-reply follow-ups 6-03, Kyle Van Pelt 8 days stale, Joe Wargo Aptive 7 days stale]\ntags: [productivity, workflow, email, communication, triage, baylor-athletics, inbox]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-03.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-03\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-06-03 Run)\n\nOn 2026-06-03, a morning Outlook inbox triage produced a structured JSON categorizing pending communications into needs-reply, follow-ups, meetings to schedule, forms owed, still-open, and FYI buckets. The run is an instance of the recurring [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]]; its two highest-urgency items were both overdue obligations carried forward from the prior day — a Catapult Sports renewal (3 days past its 5/31 due date) and a Playfly LLC timecard (4 days overdue).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Two overdue obligations as top priority:** the Catapult Sports renewal (due 5/31, now 3 days past) and the Playfly LLC timecard verification in isolved (period 5/17–5/30, 4 days overdue) were both flagged URGENT — the age × importance heuristic surfacing compliance/vendor items whose consequences escalate daily ahead of aged relationship replies.\n- **Distribution:** 6 needs-reply, 2 stale follow-ups, 2 meetings to schedule, 2 forms owed, 2 still-open threads, and 4 FYI items.\n- **Needs-reply items:** Austin Lewis (1080 Sprint demo — Jack Ertz on campus 6/2 @10:30, possibly already past), Luke Bogus (Dropback path decision — demo with sport-specific GMs vs. share with Aaron/Jake), Brandon Miller (career inquiry, U Arkansas grad), Joe Wargo (Aptive Index review meeting, 7 days stale), and Lauren Green (onboarding/password-reset confirmation).\n- **Stale follow-up:** Kyle Van Pelt calendar invite now 8 days stale — the same thread flagged at 7 days on the 2026-06-02 run, confirming continued non-response.\n- **Meetings and forms:** Courteney Tompkins (2025 Performance Clinic attendance) and Laurel Proulx (Female Athlete Pelvic Floor survey planning call) to schedule; PFF translations submission tracked toward a 7/1 deadline (not yet urgent).\n\n## Details\n\nThe 2026-06-03 triage is a near-continuation of the [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-02]] run one day earlier: the Catapult renewal, Playfly timecard, 1080 Sprint demo, Kyle Van Pelt calendar invite, Joe Wargo Aptive Index, Brandon Miller career inquiry, and Lauren Green onboarding threads all recur, with the overdue counters incremented by a day (Catapult 2→3 days, Playfly 2→4 days, Kyle Van Pelt 7→8 days stale). This day-over-day persistence demonstrates the methodology's core value: a categorized snapshot makes accumulating communication debt visible, and the age-based sort within each category keeps escalating obligations from being masked by newer arrivals. The Catapult renewal is another manifestation of the recurring Catapult OpenField vendor friction across Baylor projects.\n\nThe triage was the first of three sessions that day; the later sessions (14:45 and ~15:00) pivoted to Claude Code environment maintenance — diagnosing a missing `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` for the GitHub MCP plugin and resolving it via `setx`, which in turn surfaced the Windows stale-environment gotcha. Those maintenance findings are documented in [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] and [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] respectively.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]] — The structured 6-category audit methodology this run instantiates; the needs-reply/follow-ups/meetings/forms bucketing and age × importance prioritization applied here are defined there.\n- [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-02]] — The prior-day run sharing nearly all of this run's threads; comparing the two shows overdue counters incrementing day over day.\n- [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] — The Catapult renewal item is another instance of recurring Catapult OpenField vendor friction across Baylor projects.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The later 2026-06-03 sessions (14:45, ~15:00) that handled the GitHub MCP plugin `/doctor` warning on the same day.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-03.md]] — Inbox triage session (09:36): 6 needs-reply, 2 stale follow-ups, 2 meetings to schedule, 2 forms owed, 2 still-open, 4 FYI; Catapult renewal (3 days overdue) and Playfly timecard (4 days overdue) flagged URGENT; Kyle Van Pelt 8 days stale, Joe Wargo Aptive 7 days stale, PFF translations due 7/1 tracked.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stale-memory-router-points-at-wrong-answer.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"A Router Pointing at a Wrong Answer: Stale DEV Memory After the Mac Migration\"\naliases: [project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md, DEV lived at Desktop DEV, router pointing at wrong answer, Windows-era memory claims, eight stale DEV memory files, delegation amplifies staleness]\ntags: [memory, claude-code, migration, macos, windows, gotcha, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-02.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-02\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# A Router Pointing at a Wrong Answer: Stale DEV Memory After the Mac Migration\n\nThe sharpest finding of the 2026-09-02 memlint run was structural rather than factual. `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md` still claimed the `DEV` directory lived at `~/Desktop/DEV` — a Windows-era layout — while `DEV/CLAUDE.md` explicitly routed all layout questions *to that file*. The wrong fact was therefore not merely present in memory; it was the designated authority on its own topic. Roughly eight DEV memory files carried similar Windows-era claims, and all of them loaded into every session.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Delegation multiplies the cost of a stale fact.** A wrong claim sitting in an unreferenced file is one bad data point; a wrong claim in a file that another document names as the canonical source is the answer to every question on that topic.\n- **The stale claim survived the platform that produced it.** `~/Desktop/DEV` was correct on Windows and became wrong on macOS, with nothing in the migration path that would notice or flag the discrepancy.\n- **Scale matters: ~8 DEV memory files carried Windows-era claims, and they loaded every session.** Unlike a stale file on disk that is read only when opened, memory-tier staleness is paid on every conversation.\n- **Establishing a canonical pointer creates an obligation to verify the target.** `DEV/CLAUDE.md`'s routing line was itself correct and useful; the defect was that nothing re-checked the pointed-at content after the host changed.\n- **This is what made stale memories the higher-priority cleanup** over the 23 broken `[[links]]`: a broken link fails loudly and resolves to nothing, while a stale router answers confidently and wrongly.\n\n## Details\n\nThe failure has the same root as the other Windows-artifact survivals catalogued in [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]]: a fact captured at one moment on one host, frozen into a durable artifact, and never re-verified when the host changed underneath it. What distinguishes this instance is the routing layer. Canonical-source pointers are a good pattern — they prevent the same fact from being restated in five places and drifting apart — but they concentrate risk, because the correctness of every consumer now depends on the correctness of one target. The relocation discipline in [[concepts/canonical-section-relocation-inbound-pointers]] deals with the same coupling from the other direction, where moving a canonical section leaves inbound pointers dangling; here the pointer resolves fine and delivers a wrong answer, which is strictly harder to detect.\n\nMemory-tier staleness is also more expensive than ordinary file staleness because of when it is read. A stale README costs something only when someone opens it. A stale memory file that is injected at session start is consulted implicitly in every conversation, shaping paths, assumptions, and tool invocations without ever being displayed for review. Eight such files in the DEV slug means eight Windows-era premises silently in play at the start of every DEV session. That asymmetry is the concrete argument behind the session's option-A-over-option-B choice: broken `[[links]]` degrade retrieval, whereas stale facts degrade reasoning, and the latter fails silently.\n\nThe remedy implied by the finding is not a one-time correction but a verification obligation attached to the pointer itself. Wherever one document declares another the authority on a topic, the pair should be re-checked together whenever the underlying environment changes — a migration, a directory reorganization, a tool replacement. Absent that, the routing relationship converts a single stale file into a systematically wrong answer, and the confidence with which it is delivered is proportional to how well the routing was designed.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memlint-memory-tier-cleanup]] — The session and tooling that surfaced this finding, and the fix-in-place disposition applied to it.\n- [[concepts/canonical-section-relocation-inbound-pointers]] — The complementary hazard for canonical pointers: relocation leaves inbound references dangling rather than wrong.\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — The broader class of Windows-era facts and settings that survived the Mac migration.\n- [[connections/install-time-capture-goes-stale-when-host-moves]] — The same freeze-at-capture-time failure applied to interpreter paths and SDK pins.\n- [[concepts/rounded-counts-in-context-documents]] — The counter-discipline for always-loaded context: record facts at a stability level that survives change, or date-stamp them.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — Why memory-tier staleness is paid per session rather than per read.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-02.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:35): `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md` still claiming DEV lived at `~/Desktop/DEV` while `DEV/CLAUDE.md` routes layout questions to it — \"a router pointing at a wrong answer\"; ~8 DEV memory files carrying Windows-era claims loaded every session; stale memories prioritized over the 23 broken `[[links]]`.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Cross-Machine Push Collisions During a Session\"\naliases: [three pushes from Windows mid-session, merge on every push, something running unattended, apply global skill audit commit, 913 files deleted 160 to 63 skills]\ntags: [git, sync, cross-machine, session-continuity, workflow, windows, macos]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-14\n---\n\n# Cross-Machine Push Collisions During a Session\n\nThree separate pushes arrived from the Windows laptop during the 2026-09-14 Mac session, each requiring a merge on the Mac side. One of them — `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` — was the artifact that set the session's agenda; another, timestamped 12:12 and titled \"apply global skill audit,\" deleted 913 files and cut the global skills pool from roughly 160 to 63. The recorded observation is that it is worth knowing whether something is running unattended on the other machine.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Three pushes in one session is a rate, and the rate is the signal.** Occasional cross-machine pushes are expected; a steady arrival during a single sitting suggests an automated or agent-driven writer rather than a person at a keyboard.\n- **Every arrival cost a merge.** The friction is not the merge itself but that the Mac session's working state was repeatedly interrupted by content it did not author.\n- **The largest arrival was a 913-file deletion.** It was applied and pushed because it appears deliberate, and because git retains every deleted skill if recovery is needed — the destructive action is recoverable, so the safe move is to accept and verify rather than block.\n- **One of the pushes was the session's most useful input.** The same channel that caused the collisions delivered `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md`, so the answer is not to close the channel.\n- **The open question was recorded rather than resolved:** confirm the skill audit was intentional; nothing is lost either way.\n\n## Details\n\nTwo machines pushing to the same repositories is the intended topology — it is what makes the shared router files, the memory tier, and the cross-machine handoff queue work at all. The new information here is what that topology feels like when the other end is active: a session that expects to be the sole writer finds itself merging three times, and each merge is a small decision about content whose provenance and intent are not visible from this side. The \"apply global skill audit\" commit is the extreme case, since a 913-file deletion arriving unannounced is indistinguishable, at the git layer, between a deliberate audit and a misfired script. What made it safe to accept is a property of the substrate rather than of the commit: git retains deleted content, so accepting is reversible and blocking would only delay the same decision. That is the same reversibility calculus that made the 306 → 208 skills prune a *move* rather than a delete in [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]].\n\nThe unattended-activity question is worth asking because it changes how other signals should be read. If an automated process is pushing from the Windows machine, then a repo that appears behind may self-correct, a divergence error may self-clear, and an unfamiliar file may have an owner other than the last human session — all conditions previously observed and recorded in [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] and [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]]. In a single-writer world, an unexplained artifact is an anomaly to investigate; in a multi-writer world, it is first a question about which writer produced it. The practical consequence is that the \"check whether another session already handled this\" step stops being an occasional courtesy and becomes the default first move.\n\nThere is a specific tension in this particular session worth naming. The Windows machine was simultaneously discovered to have *no functioning scheduled automation* — nine disabled tasks and two triggerless ones — and to be pushing three times in an afternoon. Those facts do not contradict each other (interactive agent sessions push; scheduled backups do not), but together they sharpen the question: something over there is active, and it is not the backup.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — The same Windows machine, whose scheduled automation is entirely dead while its interactive pushes continue.\n- [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] — The verification posture applied to content arriving through this same channel.\n- [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] — The skills-pool work this 913-file audit further reduces; the reversible-move discipline it established.\n- [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] — \"Another session may have fixed it\" as a recorded action item rather than an assumption.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — Self-clearing divergence errors, a direct consequence of another writer being active.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The shared-repo topology that makes both the collisions and the handoffs possible.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — Why a sync indicator says less than it appears to when writers are plural.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): three separate pushes arriving from the Windows laptop during the session, each requiring a merge, with the note that it is worth knowing whether something is running unattended there; a commit \"apply global skill audit\" (12:12, authored by the user from the other machine) deleting 913 files and cutting skills from ~160 to 63 plus writing `project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md`, applied and pushed on the Mac, appearing deliberate with git retaining every deleted skill; action item to confirm the audit was intentional.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/memlint-memory-tier-cleanup.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"memlint Memory-Tier Cleanup: Fix in Place, Archive with Provenance\"\naliases: [memlint.py, memory tier lint, stale memory cleanup, 12 files fixed 5 archived, contradictions 34 to 17, structural report filename collision, DEV Windows-era memory claims, report-only linter]\ntags: [memory, claude-code, maintenance, tooling, lint, macos, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-02.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-02\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# memlint Memory-Tier Cleanup: Fix in Place, Archive with Provenance\n\nOn 2026-09-02 a memory-hygiene session built and ran `memlint.py` — a health checker for the memory files the harness injects into every session — then acted on its findings. Twelve memory files were corrected in place, five were archived into `_archive/` folders with provenance READMEs, and the LLM contradiction count dropped from 34 to 17. The session established the tool's operating constraint (report only, never edit a memory file) and the disposition rule that separates repair from removal.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`memlint.py` reports; a human or an explicitly-authorized session edits.** Merging and deleting memories stays a human decision, so the linter's output is a work list rather than an action.\n- **Fix in place beats delete.** Twelve files were corrected rather than removed: a wrong fact gets replaced with the right one, and a behavior preference whose *mechanism* has gone stale gets its mechanism repaired while the preference is preserved. Deleting would discard the still-valid intent along with the broken detail.\n- **Archive, don't hard-delete.** Only five files were retired, each moved into an `_archive/` folder with a provenance README explaining why, and their index pointers pulled from `MEMORY.md`. Nothing was destroyed.\n- **Stale memories were chosen over broken links as the higher-priority fix** because stale files actively feed wrong facts into every session, whereas a broken `[[link]]` merely fails to resolve. Wrong beats missing in cost.\n- **~17 contradictions survived by design.** About 8 of the remainder sit in files the user deliberately kept, and two FaceComps files in the `~` slug duplicate each other while spelling a coach's name two ways — a judgment call a linter cannot make.\n- **Every LLM-flagged conflict was verified against reality before an edit was made**, and the facts checked out; the flag was treated as a hypothesis, not a verdict.\n\n## Details\n\nThe disposition rule — fix in place, archive rarely, delete never — reflects what a memory file actually is. It is not a cache of facts that can be regenerated on demand; it is a compressed record of decisions, preferences, and hard-won corrections, and its wording often encodes reasoning that no longer exists anywhere else. A file whose stated mechanism has gone stale (a path that moved, a tool that was replaced) usually still carries a valid preference underneath, so the repair is surgical: replace the mechanism, keep the intent. This mirrors the routing discipline in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]], where the value of the memory tier lies in durable rules and pointers rather than in re-storable content — and it is why compression under cap pressure, as in [[concepts/memory-cap-relief-by-migration-not-deletion]], is preferred to dropping entries.\n\nThe archival half of the procedure is the same shape as the error-log discipline in [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]]: confirm staleness against an independent source, preserve the entries somewhere retrievable, then remove them from the channel that is read at session start. The provenance README is the piece that makes the archive useful rather than a graveyard — six months later, \"why is this file here and not loaded?\" has a written answer. Pulling the `MEMORY.md` index pointers at the same time is what actually stops the file from loading; leaving a pointer to an archived file would produce the index-drift condition memlint itself checks for.\n\nThe residual 17 contradictions are worth reading as a feature of the process rather than an incomplete job. Roughly eight are in files the user chose to keep, which means the linter and the owner disagree and the owner wins. The two FaceComps files in the `~` slug are the clearest case: they duplicate each other and spell a coach's name two different ways, which is exactly the kind of question — which spelling is correct, and which file should survive — that requires knowing the person, not comparing strings. The session's final discipline point is that the LLM check's flags were verified against reality before any file was touched; a contradiction detector reports that two claims cannot both be true, not which one is false, and acting on the flag without checking would risk correcting the correct claim.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The three tiers memlint audits; the routing logic that determines which content belongs in a memory file at all.\n- [[concepts/memory-cap-relief-by-migration-not-deletion]] — The complementary capacity-side discipline: relieve pressure by moving content, not by dropping it.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The same confirm-then-archive-then-clear procedure applied to the `.errors.log` escalation channel.\n- [[concepts/stale-memory-router-points-at-wrong-answer]] — The specific and worst finding from this run: a routing pointer aimed at a file whose answer was wrong.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The sibling health check for the automation stack; memlint extends the same periodic-sweep discipline to the memory tier.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — An earlier single-pass triage of flagged health-check items on the same stack.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-02.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:35): option A (stale memories) chosen over option B (broken links) because stale files actively feed wrong facts; 12 files fixed in place, 5 archived into `_archive/` with provenance READMEs and index pointers pulled from `MEMORY.md`; `memlint.py` constrained to report-only; contradictions 34 → 17 with ~8 remaining in deliberately-kept files; two duplicate FaceComps files in the `~` slug with two spellings of a coach's name left for a human; LLM-flagged conflicts verified against reality before editing.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\"\naliases: [three-tier memory, Tier A memory, Tier B scratchpad, Stop hook per-turn, memory tiers, claude memory layers, hook firing mechanics, Tier B near cap, project memory files, memsearch, user_career_timeline, memory routing matrix, what not to store in memory]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, automation, hooks, infrastructure, architecture]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-05.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-19\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\n\nThe Claude Code memory system is composed of three independent layers, each operating at a different granularity and owned by a different mechanism. This architecture was articulated on 2026-05-19 during a session that traced exactly when and how each capture mechanism fires. On 2026-06-05, a practical capacity-management routing pattern emerged for when Tier B global memory approaches its character cap. On 2026-06-18, a worked example clarified how to route a specific piece of context across the tiers — and which content should not enter the memory system at all.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Tier A — Native Claude Code auto-memory:** Built into Claude Code itself; managed per-project; no user configuration required. Operates automatically within a project context; its exact retention and synthesis mechanics are not user-visible.\n- **Tier B — Custom global scratchpad via Stop hook:** A user-configured hook on the `Stop` event, which fires after every conversation turn. This provides incremental, per-turn transcript capture to a global scratchpad file (MEMORY.md) — a granularity that neither Tier A nor Tier C provides.\n- **Tier C — claude-memory-compiler:** The [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] system: `SessionEnd` and `PreCompact` hooks fire shell commands at session teardown or auto-compaction, spawning a detached `flush.py` process that appends structured notes to the daily log; at end-of-day these are compiled into knowledge articles.\n- **The agent never sees a \"session ending\" event.** Session end and compaction are harness-level events; it is the Claude Code harness that runs the hook shell commands, not the agent itself.\n- **PreCompact as a safety net:** In a long session with multiple auto-compactions, PreCompact fires before each one, preventing total context loss if the session later crashes before `SessionEnd`.\n- **When Tier B is near cap, route to Tier A project files:** The global MEMORY.md (Tier B) has a character-cap constraint. When it approaches capacity, new durable facts should be saved to Tier A project-specific memory files (e.g., `project_<name>.md`, `user_career_timeline.md`), which are cross-project searchable via memsearch.\n- **Content-to-tier routing matrix (2026-06-18):** file locations and versioning decisions belong in Tier B as an active-thread entry (\"continue this next session\"); durable personal conventions (e.g. \"always save v1 before editing\") belong in Tier A as a feedback rule; and the actual artifact content (slides, scripts) should NOT be duplicated in memory at all because it already lives on disk. Tier C's daily-log capture provides a compressed \"what did I do that day\" summary but is not a substitute for a durable artifact reference.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three tiers are designed to capture context at different time horizons and granularities, and they operate independently rather than hierarchically. Tier A is fully opaque to the user. Tier B's Stop-hook scratchpad is the most granular: because the Stop event fires after every conversation turn, Tier B can capture mid-session context incrementally in near-real-time. Tier C produces the richest synthesized output — compiled knowledge articles from daily logs — but fires only at session boundaries.\n\nThe independence of the three tiers is their key reliability property. A failure in one does not cascade to the others: if a session crashes before `SessionEnd` fires, the Stop-hook's Tier B scratchpad may still contain the session's important turns. The \"agent never sees session ending\" detail is architecturally important: code written inside the agent context cannot detect or react to its own session ending. Teardown logic must be placed in hook shell commands that the harness executes.\n\nThe Tier B near-cap routing pattern (2026-06-05) addresses a practical capacity constraint. The correct response when Tier B is near capacity is not to compress existing entries — which risks discarding information encoded in current phrasing — but to route new durable facts into named Tier A project memory files, which are surfaced by memsearch across all project contexts.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 worked example makes the routing decision concrete and, importantly, identifies content that should stay out of memory entirely. Asked how best to preserve a working session's context (an RTP presentation's file locations, a v1/v2 versioning decision, and the slides/script themselves), the assistant produced a routing matrix rather than dumping everything into one tier: the file paths and versioning state map to Tier B because they describe an active thread to resume next session; a general preference like \"always save v1 before editing\" maps to Tier A as a durable feedback rule; and the actual slide and script content maps to neither — it already exists as files on disk, so copying it into memory would only create a stale duplicate. The distinction matters because the memory system's value is in cheap-to-retrieve pointers and rules, not in re-storing artifacts that a file path already addresses. The same example noted that Tier C's automatic daily-log capture is a lossy summary (\"good for what did I do that day, not great for pick up exactly where I left off\"), reinforcing that a deliberate Tier B pointer is the right tool when the goal is precise session continuity.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — Detailed installation and configuration guide for Tier C.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — A downstream consumer of Tier C's compiled daily-log output.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — Maintenance pass on the same Claude Code environment that houses all three memory tiers.\n- [[concepts/end-of-day-structured-interview]] — Documents the USER.md 1,375-char cap and the Tier A vs. Tier B (durable vs. active) distinction.\n- [[concepts/weekly-review-synthesis-workflow]] — The end-of-week close-out workflow where near-cap Tier B → Tier A routing was first observed in practice.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-19.md]] — Three-tier architecture explanation, hook firing order, and \"agent never sees session ending\" clarification (16:53).\n- [[daily/2026-06-05.md]] — Tier B near-cap → Tier A project files routing pattern observed during end-of-week memory housekeeping (18:21).\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Content-to-tier routing worked example (01:29): file locations/versioning → Tier B active thread, \"always save v1\" → Tier A feedback rule, actual artifact content → left on disk, not duplicated in memory.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/automation-stack-doctor.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\"\naliases: [stack doctor, KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck, post-restart health check, self-deleting scheduled task, nightly self-maintaining cycle, daily-backup.ps1, backup execution limit, cloud-check UTC bug, stack doctor ALL CLEAR]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, windows, task-scheduler, memory, infrastructure, maintenance, scheduling, powershell]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-27.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-27\nupdated: 2026-08-02\n---\n\n# Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\n\nThe automation stack doctor is a health-check routine that verifies the full local Claude Code automation and memory stack — scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus, the error log, backups, and the Google Drive API heartbeat — and reports a consolidated status. On 2026-07-27, following a Windows reboot, a post-restart stack doctor run returned ALL CLEAR, and the session restructured the stack into a self-maintaining nightly cycle: heavy reindexing was moved into the 6:15 AM backup task while the session-end hook stays a light 45-second incremental pass.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Stack doctor scope:** a single run validates all 4 scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus survival across restart, error-log cleanliness, backup freshness, and a Google Drive API heartbeat. The 2026-07-27 post-restart run returned ALL CLEAR (backups fresh at 3h, Milvus survived reboot).\n- **Self-deleting post-restart check:** a one-shot scheduled task (`KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck`, triggered at logon + 5 min) auto-runs stack doctor after a reboot, escalates any failures to `.errors.log`, and deletes itself. This is the workaround for the fact that a session cannot literally wait through a machine restart — failures surface at the next session start via the error log.\n- **Nightly reindex folded into the 6:15 AM backup task** (`daily-backup.ps1`): uncapped, uses the full `memsearch.exe` path (per the Task Scheduler PATH lesson), escalates per-path failures, and resets the hook timeout-streak counter on success. Rationale: a first-time SecondBrain book-library index is an hours-scale job that a 45-second session-end hook can never absorb.\n- **Backup task execution limit raised 30 min → 2 h** so a heavy reindex night is not killed mid-pass. The session-end hook stays light (45 s incremental) and its alarm threshold was raised 3 → 6 so it only alarms if the nightly safety net itself falls behind — a real signal, not noise.\n- **Cloud-check timezone bug fixed:** a \"-4h old\" false backup-freshness reading came from timestamps not normalized to UTC after `ConvertFrom-Json`; the check now normalizes to UTC regardless of the return type.\n\n## Details\n\nThe session's central move is an architectural relocation of expensive work off the interactive/hook path and onto a scheduled job. The recurring memsearch indexing alarm had been misdiagnosed the prior day as an \"occasional\" hiccup; on 2026-07-27 it was recognized as structural — a first-time embedding job measured in hours (one indexer had burned 81 CPU-minutes and was still running). Because a 45-second session-end hook can never complete an hours-scale index, the correct home for that work is the nightly 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1` task, which runs uncapped and escalates per-path failures. The session-end hook is left as a light incremental pass, and its alarm threshold is raised from 3 to 6 consecutive misses so it only fires when the nightly safety net itself falls behind. With the library index converging over a night or two while search stays live, the alarm becomes a meaningful signal rather than chronic noise.\n\nTwo supporting reliability fixes accompanied the restructure. The backup task's execution time limit was raised from 30 minutes to 2 hours so a heavy reindex night is not terminated mid-pass — a scheduled job that runs an unbounded index needs an execution ceiling wide enough to absorb the worst case. The cloud-check UTC normalization fix addressed a false \"-4h old\" reading: after `ConvertFrom-Json`, timestamps were being compared without normalizing to UTC, so a local-vs-UTC offset was misread as staleness; normalizing regardless of the parsed return type eliminated the false alarm. The `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting task closes the post-reboot verification gap — because no session can survive a restart, a one-shot logon-triggered task that runs stack doctor and escalates to `.errors.log` is the mechanism by which post-restart health reaches the user at the next session start. All changes were committed and pushed to the dotclaude and skills repos with no pending user action; the first fully self-maintaining cycle (backup → snapshot → reindex → alarm reset) was scheduled to run the following morning at 6:15 AM.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The memsearch tool whose hours-scale first-index drove this restructure; the nightly reindex-in-backup-task architecture and the raised 3→6 alarm threshold are documented there as well.\n- [[concepts/windows-task-scheduler-path-config]] — The full-`memsearch.exe`-path requirement applied to the nightly reindex task is a direct application of this Task Scheduler minimal-PATH lesson.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — The architectural rationale for moving hours-scale reindexing out of the session entirely: long-running background commands do not survive a session, so scheduled jobs are their correct home.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — An earlier single-pass health-check-and-remediation session on the same stack; the stack doctor generalizes that periodic triage discipline into an automated, scheduled routine.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-07-27.md]] — Post-restart stack doctor session (16:40): ALL CLEAR result, `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting logon task registered, nightly reindex moved into 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1`, session-end hook left at 45 s with alarm threshold raised 3→6, backup execution limit raised 30 min→2 h, cloud-check UTC normalization bug fixed, PR #16 merged (`59b6f2c`).\n\n```\n\n### connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Windows Defaults Outlive the Windows Machine\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine\"\n  - \"concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas\"\n  - \"concepts/codex-path-rewrite-incident\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-08.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-08\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# Connection: Windows Defaults Outlive the Windows Machine\n\n## The Connection\n\nA Windows→Mac migration moves the files but not the assumptions baked into them. Three distinct artifacts from the Windows era were still actively causing failures on macOS on 2026-08-08: `core.symlinks=false` in `.git/config`, inherited because Windows requires elevated privileges to create symlinks and git sets the flag automatically; Windows-shaped paths embedded in scripts (`~\\.Codex\\scripts\\daily-backup.ps1`, with backslashes); and the CRLF/`autocrlf` normalization behavior documented earlier in the worktree gotchas. Each was a correct, even necessary, accommodation on the original platform. None was re-evaluated when the platform changed.\n\n## Key Insight\n\n**Platform-specific settings are written once at install or clone time and then become invisible, so they survive migrations that invalidate their justification.** `core.symlinks=false` is the sharpest case: on Windows it prevented git from attempting symlink creation it could not perform, which was correct. On macOS, where symlinks work natively, the same setting is purely destructive — it makes every `checkout`, `reset`, and `stash` write 21 tracked symlinks out as 29-byte text files, silently breaking all 21 mattpocock skills. The setting did not fail; it did exactly what it says. What failed is that nothing in the migration process asked whether it should still be set.\n\nThe second-order insight is that macOS's case-insensitive filesystem actively *concealed* one of these artifacts. Because `.Codex` and `.codex` resolve to the same directory, the mangled path references produced no filesystem error and no failed resolution — the bug was detectable only by reading text, never by testing whether a path worked. A migration artifact that produces a loud error gets fixed on day one; one that produces no error at all can persist for months. This inverts the usual intuition that a more permissive destination platform is safer: macOS's permissiveness about case is precisely what let the defect hide.\n\nThe practical consequence is that a cross-platform migration needs an explicit audit of *carried-over configuration*, distinct from the file migration itself. The audit list this incident suggests: per-repo `.git/config` values that git sets platform-conditionally (`core.symlinks`, `core.autocrlf`, `core.longpaths`), embedded path separators and drive letters in scripts, and any case-sensitivity assumption that the source filesystem enforced and the destination does not (or vice versa — a Mac→Linux move would newly *expose* case bugs rather than hide them).\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-08-08: \"`core.symlinks=false` (inherited from Windows), any future `checkout`/`reset`/`stash` writes them as 29-byte text files and re-breaks all 21 skills. It's a recurring landmine.\" The setting was still active on the Mac checkout months after migration.\n- 2026-08-08: the `.Codex` survey initially missed `~\\.Codex\\scripts\\daily-backup.ps1` — a Windows-backslash path shape that a POSIX-oriented grep pattern does not match, contributing to the 29-vs-235 scope undercount.\n- 2026-08-08: \"macOS case-insensitivity hid `.Codex` vs `.codex` resolving to the same directory\" — the destination platform's permissiveness suppressed the error signal that would have surfaced the bug.\n- 2026-05-22 / 2026-06-17: Windows `core.autocrlf=true` produced phantom diffs and false-positive file-divergence comparisons, resolved with `git update-index --refresh` — the same class of platform-conditional git config causing confusion after the fact.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]]\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]]\n- [[concepts/codex-path-rewrite-incident]]\n- [[connections/windows-process-env-fixed-at-launch]]\n- [[connections/install-time-capture-goes-stale-when-host-moves]]\n\n```\n\n### concepts/at-utilization-benchmarks-project.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"AT Utilization Benchmarks Project (Baylor Athletic Training / Kitman)\"\naliases: [AT_Utilization_Benchmarks, Kitman utilization, AT utilization metrics, Metric Dictionary, build_metric_dictionary_docx, vendor-mediated data access, Kitman API locked, one-shot request spec, AT utilization reporting, python-docx AT deliverable, Carrie alignment, utilization brief, 17 Baylor sports, complete coverage brief]\ntags: [baylor-athletics, athletic-training, kitman, data-pipeline, project-management, python, deliverables, vendor-mediation]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-09.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-09\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# AT Utilization Benchmarks Project (Baylor Athletic Training / Kitman)\n\nOn 2026-06-09, the AT_Utilization_Benchmarks project was built out following two back-to-back meetings — \"EOY Kitman Utilization\" (Jun 8) and \"Athletic Training — Utilization Metrics Standardization\" (Jun 9). The session produced a humanized Metric Dictionary v2 with a rebuildable Word deliverable via `python-docx`, and surfaced a critical constraint: Kitman API access is fully vendor-mediated, requiring a fundamental shift in data strategy from iterative incremental pulls to a single comprehensive, precisely-specified request. On 2026-06-18, a cleanup pass on the utilization brief resolved an outstanding \"missing 18th sport\" question — Baylor sponsors 17 varsity sports, so the brief already covered 100% of programs — and corrected the brief's stored file path.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Metric Dictionary v1 → v2 (humanizer pass):** removed em-dashes, bold-label scaffolding, and template-rhythm artifacts from v1; v2 reads as a practitioner document rather than a template output. A Word version was built via `python-docx` (`scripts/build_metric_dictionary_docx.py`) with Baylor green headings — pandoc is unavailable in this environment, making the direct Python approach the only scriptable option. The script is rebuildable from source on demand.\n- **Kitman API lockout — vendor-mediated only:** no direct API access exists at this contract tier. All data pulls flow through the Kitman vendor, killing iterative request cycles. Strategic implication: design one comprehensive, precise data request rather than requesting incrementally. Every field omitted costs a full vendor-mediation round trip. The access constraint also doubles as contract-renegotiation evidence: 17 months remain on the Kitman contract, and paying license fees while needing vendor mediation for access to one's own data is a concrete negotiating point.\n- **One-shot request spec design:** target: all teams, CSV format (not PDF), covering treatment, injury, availability, and roster data. The spec must enumerate every field and filter needed across all downstream use cases before submission — second requests for missed fields represent full round trips in calendar time.\n- **Carrie as gatekeeper and alignment partner:** emailing Carrie the Metric Dictionary docx both advances the project (her response gates downstream work) and prompts her to share her own documentation materials. Strategy for incoming docs: alignment pass, not a heavier rulebook — adopt her language where it overlaps the five core metrics, keep genuinely new asks minimal to reduce adoption friction.\n- **AGENTS.md + CLAUDE.md pointer shim as default pattern:** the project was configured with `AGENTS.md` as the harness-agnostic source of truth and `CLAUDE.md` as a one-line pointer shim. This was explicitly saved to persistent memory as the user's preferred default for all future projects.\n- **Baylor sponsors 17 varsity sports (2026-06-18):** a \"missing 18th sport\" open item on the utilization brief was resolved by confirming the roster — Baylor does not offer beach volleyball, so the correct count is 17, and the brief already covered 100% of Baylor's programs (17/17). The brief's stored file path was corrected from `D:\\Dev\\Programs\\...` to `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Documents\\...`, and the open \"which team is uncovered?\" question was removed as resolved.\n- **Sensitivity rules:** trainer names are anonymized in all outbound documents; athlete medical data in `data/` stays local and is never included in shareable outputs or committed to remote repositories.\n\n## Details\n\nThe Kitman API constraint is the most architecturally consequential finding from this project. Unlike the Catapult and Perch integrations — where an API exists but behaves unexpectedly from first assumptions (see [[connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction]]) — Kitman offers no API access at all for this contract tier. This eliminates the typical iterative data-exploration workflow (request, inspect, identify gaps, re-request) and replaces it with a single-shot specification design problem. The analyst must enumerate every field, filter, team scope, and format needed across all downstream use cases before submitting the request, because each follow-up is a vendor-mediated round trip measured in calendar days rather than API response times. The framing of Kitman's vendor mediation as contract-renegotiation evidence is strategically useful — making the case for API access in the next contract cycle is strengthened when the gap between paying fees and needing vendor help to access one's own data is documented concretely.\n\nThe `python-docx` deliverable pattern establishes a reusable technique for environments where the standard document-conversion pipeline (pandoc) is unavailable. The `scripts/build_metric_dictionary_docx.py` script takes structured metric definitions and produces a formatted `.docx` with organizational branding, making the deliverable reproducible from source. Word was chosen over PDF as the primary deliverable format because AT staff and clinical collaborators are more likely to annotate, forward, and edit a `.docx` than a PDF.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 cleanup pass illustrates a small but generalizable data-hygiene lesson: when a count discrepancy appears in a deliverable (\"the table shows 17 sports but Baylor has 18 — which one is missing?\"), the correct first move is to verify the source roster rather than assume the table has a gap. Here the apparent gap did not exist — Baylor sponsors exactly 17 varsity sports and does not field beach volleyball, so the brief was already complete at 17/17. Confirming the roster both closed the open question and prevented a spurious \"add the missing sport\" edit that would have introduced an error. The same pass corrected the brief's stored path (the memory entry pointed to a stale `D:\\Dev\\Programs\\...` location rather than the actual `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Documents\\...` file), synchronizing the daily log and memory record with the on-disk reality.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files]] — The AGENTS.md + CLAUDE.md pointer-shim adopted for this project is the import-approach variant of the dual-context-file pattern.\n- [[connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction]] — The Kitman vendor-mediation constraint is one pattern in Baylor's sports-tech integration landscape alongside Catapult's token complexity, Perch's auth-scheme surprises, and Kinexon's CSM-gated docs; all share the property that naive integration assumptions fail.\n- [[concepts/bu-cheer-summer-2026-training]] — Another Baylor Athletics deliverable where `python-docx`/`openpyxl` were chosen because standard conversion tools were unavailable; both produce rebuildable, script-driven document artifacts.\n- [[concepts/tezino-camp-prep-spc-monitoring]] — A concurrent Baylor project also advancing athlete monitoring capability; the two represent complementary layers of an emerging applied performance analytics stack.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-09.md]] — AT utilization session (17:13): metric dictionary humanizer pass, python-docx deliverable and rebuild script, Kitman API lockout discovery, one-shot request spec strategy, Carrie alignment pass approach, AGENTS.md default preference saved to persistent memory, sensitivity rules codified.\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Utilization-brief cleanup (09:37, 10:43): resolved the \"missing 18th sport\" open item (Baylor sponsors 17 varsity sports; no beach volleyball; brief already 17/17 complete), corrected the brief's stored file path to `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Documents\\...`, removed the resolved open question.\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 285) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-20.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-20\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (16:58)\n\n\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-20.md | 2026-09-21 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-21T18:30:12-05:00] compile | 2026-09-20.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-20.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-20.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
